draw the game straight out under a hud

The volume HUD sent every frame through the canvas while it showed: the game
into the canvas, the HUD over it, and the whole 752x560 canvas copied to the
panel. Measured on the Flip in Super Mario World, render went from ~10 ms to
21.6 ms, past the 16.7 ms period, so with the core driven by the panel the
game slowed for as long as the bar was up (0031 made up the speed; the
dropped frames stayed).

A display list that is the game followed only by sprites now keeps the
direct path: the game is scaled straight onto the panel as before, and the
sprites are composed on the canvas over just the rect they cover -- the
surround and the game's own rows under it, drawn into the canvas with the
game pass limited to those rows -- and that rect alone is copied out. The
panel is never read back. With blue light on, whose gain the canvas copy
applies, it keeps the canvas path.

A test draws the same game and HUD both ways, upright and mirrored, and
requires the panel to match the canvas path pixel for pixel; it finds 5,400
pixels wrong with the overlay step left out.

Applies on top of 0031, upstream 91499a9.

